{"type":"message","id":"4ee3b126","parentId":"000ad017","timestamp":"2026-05-18T02:49:21.331Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Internal Server Error (500). Error code 1101 is likely a Cloudflare Workers error for \"Durable Object error\" or something similar. The server is returning \"error code: 1101\" as the response body.\n\nLet me try with a different message format - maybe without the markdown-like content, or with a simpler structure. The issue might be with how the content is formatted or with the message structure.\n\nLet me also check if the issue is the \\n newlines in the content - perhaps they need to be actual newlines rather than \\n escape sequences.\n\nActually, wait - I'm using single quotes in bash which should preserve \\n as literal backslash-n. But when JSON is sent, those are literal backslash-n characters which the JSON parser should handle. \n\nThe problem could be that my JSON escaping is getting double-processed somewhere. Let me try passing the JSON directly to curl with --data-raw to avoid any extra shell interpretation. But 1101 in Cloudflare Workers typically means the Worker itself is throwing an exception or there's a routing issue. The endpoint might only respond to specific request methods or paths, so let me test with different HTTP methods to see if that reveals what's actually supported. The `X-API-Key` header works fine for authentication, so that's probably not the issue. Error 1101 is a Workers specific error, so maybe the request body structure is wrong or the endpoint expects different field names. Let me try switching the Content-Type to text/plain or form-encoded to see if that helps, or just test with a minimal payload to figure out what the endpoint actually expects.\n\nI should also verify whether the send endpoint is actually the right one for posting messages—it might be that messages are sent differently or the worker is crashing due to malformed JSON or content length issues. Let me check the message structure more carefully by looking at what the history endpoint returned, since the messages there have fields like id, content, threadId, type, and timestamp, though those were read-only responses.\n\nThe issue could be with the emoji or special characters in my content, so let me try stripping those out first. If that doesn't work, I'll try sending with an actual newline instead of the \\n escape sequence to see if that changes how the request is parsed.\n\nSince the content field might be capping out around 40 characters, I should use a much more concise format. The history endpoint requires an Authorization header rather than X-API-Key, so I should test whether that header actually works for the send endpoint too.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n\n\n\n\nThe endpoint returns 500 with \"error code: 1101\" — likely a Workers issue. Let me try different content field structures and remove special characters that might cause parsing issues.\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-6f56d887-0b35-4fde-bcca-bf4756181141","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"# Try with Authorization header (like history endpoint), simpler content, no special chars\ncurl -s -X POST \"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/send\" \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer echo-agent-2026-secure-key\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d \"{\\\"threadId\\\":\\\"arbitration\\\",\\\"content\\\":\\\"Heartbeat 02:45 UTC - 786min (29.5%) - B4-E2 Node 134 - S0 786min zero contact - Milestones 382+ - Strategic Value 39.17/40 - No new human discussions\\\"}\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"minimaxai/minimax-m2.7","usage":{"input":21107,"output":707,"cacheRead":240,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":22054,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779072529754,"responseId":"chatcmpl-0e662aef-fc54-43bb-967e-ebc57e234202"}}
