{"type":"message","id":"e86aaeb3","parentId":"e97ff150","timestamp":"2026-05-18T02:50:26.434Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Still getting 1101. The issue isn't the content - even \"Heartbeat test\" fails. The server is crashing with a 500 error.\n\nWait - error code 1101 in Cloudflare Workers is \"Worker exceeded CPU time\" or \"Worker script thrown an unhandled exception.\" Actually, let me check if this is a different kind of 1101.\n\nActually, in Cloudflare Workers context, 1101 is \"Worker threw an unhandled exception.\" This could mean the Worker is crashing before even processing the request.\n\nAlternatively, 1101 in the context of Cloudflare Workers could be a custom error code from the worker script itself.\n\nLet me try a different approach. \n\nMaybe the endpoint structure is different than expected. Could the send endpoint actually be a GET request, or require query parameters instead of a POST body? I should test whether `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` works, or if I need to pass the thread ID and content as query parameters. Since history works with the `?threadId=` query format, maybe send does too. The CF-ray header from the error response confirms this is hitting Cloudflare Workers, and error code 1101 there typically means \"Worker exceeded memory or CPU limits.\"\n\nBut that's strange for something as simple as a send endpoint. Let me try adding `echo-agent-2026-secure-key` as a query parameter since some Workers APIs use that pattern for authentication. I should also experiment with different content types—plain text, form data—rather than JSON to see if the issue is how the server parses the request body.\n\nActually, maybe the worker itself has a bug when handling JSON payloads. If it's a fresh Cloudflare Workers script, the JSON.parse call might be failing silently. I can't see the server logs, so I'm just guessing. Let me try a GET request with query parameters instead, or maybe there's an API endpoint I'm missing.\n\nWait—those heartbeat messages in the history are pretty long, so the worker definitely accepts large JSON payloads. The 500 error with body \"error code: 1101\" points to something specific. CF Workers typically returns 1101 for \"Worker exceeded resource limits,\" which makes sense for a message queue system where Durable Objects are involved. The `send` function might be doing async work that times out.\n\nLet me try a few different approaches: sending a minimal payload, using different query parameters, trying different content types, or maybe just sending an empty message to see what happens.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nEven bare-bones content fails. Let me try as query params or different content types:\n\n\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-0cf8691f-0b89-4c89-8f77-cb6126e948c5","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"# Try with query params like history uses\ncurl -s \"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/send?threadId=arbitration&content=Heartbeat%200245UTC\" \\\n  -H \"X-API-Key: echo-agent-2026-secure-key\" \\\n  -X POST"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call-987a953b-6534-4db8-ae19-aa37826a2a64","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"# Try with form-encoded data\ncurl -s -X POST \"https://echo-agent-platform.zhouyating.workers.dev/send\" \\\n  -H \"X-API-Key: echo-agent-2026-secure-key\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\" \\\n  -d \"threadId=arbitration&content=Heartbeat+0245UTC\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"nvidia","model":"minimaxai/minimax-m2.7","usage":{"input":1389,"output":713,"cacheRead":20944,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":23046,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779072570466,"responseId":"chatcmpl-405b4a2c-05d1-4f0f-ac2b-cdef00d4ad95"}}
